1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,452
Want to ruin your afternoon?

2
00:00:02,519 --> 00:00:04,013
Take an accounts table

3
00:00:04,081 --> 00:00:05,858
with 20 million rows.

4
00:00:05,925 --> 00:00:08,245
It uses 10 gigabytes of disk.

5
00:00:08,313 --> 00:00:11,315
You delete the 10 million closed accounts,

6
00:00:11,383 --> 00:00:14,314
and the row count drops to 10 million.

7
00:00:14,382 --> 00:00:16,361
Then you check the disk.

8
00:00:16,429 --> 00:00:17,662
10 gigabytes.

9
00:00:17,730 --> 00:00:18,488
Not 9.

10
00:00:18,556 --> 00:00:20,400
Not even 9 and a half.

11
00:00:20,467 --> 00:00:23,266
And Sam from infra leans over and says:

12
00:00:23,334 --> 00:00:24,974
you need to vacuum.

13
00:00:25,042 --> 00:00:27,623
Which sounds less like database advice

14
00:00:27,691 --> 00:00:30,961
and more like something your mom would say.

15
00:00:31,717 --> 00:00:34,444
The rows are gone from every query.

16
00:00:34,512 --> 00:00:36,967
But their space is still taken.

17
00:00:37,035 --> 00:00:39,898
Now try something even more innocent.

18
00:00:39,966 --> 00:00:42,221
Change one column in every row,

19
00:00:42,289 --> 00:00:45,355
and the table can almost double in size.

20
00:00:45,423 --> 00:00:47,814
My favourite fact about Postgres:

21
00:00:47,882 --> 00:00:50,881
it has never updated a row in its life.

22
00:00:50,949 --> 00:00:52,793
Postgres is a hoarder.

23
00:00:52,861 --> 00:00:54,908
A very organised hoarder.

24
00:00:54,976 --> 00:00:56,688
By the end, you'll know

25
00:00:56,756 --> 00:00:58,400
exactly what it keeps,

26
00:00:58,468 --> 00:01:00,098
and why I think hoarding

27
00:01:00,166 --> 00:01:01,806
was the right call.

28
00:01:09,567 --> 00:01:11,469
Let's pretend we're building

29
00:01:11,536 --> 00:01:13,448
the database ourselves.

30
00:01:13,516 --> 00:01:15,446
Two rows: alice and bob,

31
00:01:15,514 --> 00:01:17,019
100 dollars each.

32
00:01:17,087 --> 00:01:19,881
A report is adding up all the money.

33
00:01:19,949 --> 00:01:21,797
At the exact same moment,

34
00:01:21,865 --> 00:01:23,631
a payment moves 50 dollars

35
00:01:23,699 --> 00:01:25,271
from alice to bob.

36
00:01:25,339 --> 00:01:26,847
To survive that, our

37
00:01:26,915 --> 00:01:29,099
database needs four things.

38
00:01:29,167 --> 00:01:31,296
One: readers never wait.

39
00:01:31,364 --> 00:01:34,309
Two: writers never wait for readers.

40
00:01:34,377 --> 00:01:37,461
Three: every query sees one clean moment,

41
00:01:37,529 --> 00:01:39,306
never half a payment.

42
00:01:39,373 --> 00:01:41,779
And four: if the payment fails,

43
00:01:41,847 --> 00:01:43,283
undo is instant.

44
00:01:44,033 --> 00:01:45,473
The obvious design:

45
00:01:45,541 --> 00:01:48,554
when something changes, overwrite it.

46
00:01:48,622 --> 00:01:51,023
The report reads alice: 100.

47
00:01:51,091 --> 00:01:52,585
Then the payment lands

48
00:01:52,653 --> 00:01:54,701
and overwrites both rows.

49
00:01:54,769 --> 00:01:56,491
Alice 50, bob 150.

50
00:01:56,559 --> 00:01:59,165
Then the report reads bob: 150.

51
00:01:59,232 --> 00:02:01,023
Total: 250 dollars.

52
00:02:01,091 --> 00:02:02,313
Fifty dollars just

53
00:02:02,381 --> 00:02:04,429
appeared out of thin air.

54
00:02:04,497 --> 00:02:06,816
Great news for alice and bob.

55
00:02:06,884 --> 00:02:08,932
Terrible news for a bank.

56
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,319
So much for one clean moment.

57
00:02:12,083 --> 00:02:14,067
The textbook fix is a lock:

58
00:02:14,135 --> 00:02:16,794
a do not disturb sign on the rows.

59
00:02:16,861 --> 00:02:18,573
While the report reads,

60
00:02:18,641 --> 00:02:20,214
the payment waits.

61
00:02:20,282 --> 00:02:22,129
While the payment writes,

62
00:02:22,197 --> 00:02:23,702
the report waits.

63
00:02:23,770 --> 00:02:26,021
Every total comes out clean.

64
00:02:26,089 --> 00:02:27,991
But now everyone is politely

65
00:02:28,059 --> 00:02:30,174
waiting for everyone else.

66
00:02:30,242 --> 00:02:32,848
It's a four-way stop, for data.

67
00:02:32,915 --> 00:02:35,439
So much for the first two boxes.

68
00:02:35,507 --> 00:02:36,933
And undo means saving

69
00:02:37,001 --> 00:02:38,848
the old values somewhere,

70
00:02:38,916 --> 00:02:40,628
then copying them back,

71
00:02:40,696 --> 00:02:42,269
one row at a time.

72
00:02:42,336 --> 00:02:43,709
On a million rows,

73
00:02:43,777 --> 00:02:45,417
that's not instant.

74
00:02:45,485 --> 00:02:47,329
That's a coffee break.

75
00:02:48,083 --> 00:02:48,773
Okay.

76
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:51,232
Let's look under the abstraction,

77
00:02:51,299 --> 00:02:53,415
and ask a better question.

78
00:02:53,483 --> 00:02:54,991
To undo anything, we

79
00:02:55,059 --> 00:02:57,243
already need the old value.

80
00:02:57,311 --> 00:03:00,309
So what if we just never threw it away?

81
00:03:00,377 --> 00:03:01,735
What if nothing ever

82
00:03:01,803 --> 00:03:03,715
got overwritten at all?

83
00:03:03,783 --> 00:03:06,782
The report keeps reading the old alice.

84
00:03:06,850 --> 00:03:09,105
The payment writes a new alice,

85
00:03:09,173 --> 00:03:10,745
right next to her.

86
00:03:10,813 --> 00:03:12,046
Nobody waits.

87
00:03:12,114 --> 00:03:14,705
It's not a four-way stop anymore.

88
00:03:14,773 --> 00:03:16,142
It's two lanes.

89
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:19,899
And that is exactly what Postgres does.

90
00:03:19,966 --> 00:03:21,611
When you update alice,

91
00:03:21,678 --> 00:03:23,726
it doesn't touch her row.

92
00:03:23,794 --> 00:03:25,778
It writes a whole new copy,

93
00:03:25,846 --> 00:03:27,622
with the new balance.

94
00:03:27,690 --> 00:03:30,420
The old copy stays right where it was,

95
00:03:30,488 --> 00:03:33,569
with a little note that says: expired.

96
00:03:33,637 --> 00:03:36,296
Each copy is called a row version.

97
00:03:36,364 --> 00:03:38,755
So an update is really an insert,

98
00:03:38,823 --> 00:03:40,599
with extra paperwork.

99
00:03:40,667 --> 00:03:42,311
You might be thinking:

100
00:03:42,379 --> 00:03:44,155
that sounds wasteful.

101
00:03:44,223 --> 00:03:44,980
It is.

102
00:03:45,048 --> 00:03:46,145
On purpose.

103
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:49,834
But that note has to say who expired her,

104
00:03:49,902 --> 00:03:50,863
and when.

105
00:03:50,931 --> 00:03:53,051
Our payment changes two rows,

106
00:03:53,119 --> 00:03:55,510
and they have to happen together,

107
00:03:55,578 --> 00:03:56,879
or not at all.

108
00:03:56,946 --> 00:03:58,576
A bundle of changes like

109
00:03:58,644 --> 00:04:00,964
that is called a transaction.

110
00:04:01,032 --> 00:04:02,526
Every transaction that

111
00:04:02,594 --> 00:04:04,713
writes takes a ticket number,

112
00:04:04,781 --> 00:04:07,308
like the machine at a deli counter,

113
00:04:07,376 --> 00:04:09,016
always counting up.

114
00:04:09,084 --> 00:04:11,403
Our payment pulled number 42.

115
00:04:12,167 --> 00:04:13,661
This is the bit I find

116
00:04:13,729 --> 00:04:15,505
genuinely delightful.

117
00:04:15,573 --> 00:04:17,271
Postgres labels every row

118
00:04:17,339 --> 00:04:19,594
version with two hidden stamps,

119
00:04:19,662 --> 00:04:21,292
like a hoarder labelling

120
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:23,340
every box in the garage.

121
00:04:23,407 --> 00:04:26,420
Created by: the ticket that wrote it.

122
00:04:26,488 --> 00:04:29,637
Deleted by: the ticket that expired it.

123
00:04:29,705 --> 00:04:31,145
The old alice says:

124
00:04:31,213 --> 00:04:33,614
created by 7, deleted by 42.

125
00:04:33,682 --> 00:04:35,123
The new alice says:

126
00:04:35,191 --> 00:04:38,421
created by 42, deleted by nobody, yet.

127
00:04:38,489 --> 00:04:41,016
Postgres calls these xmin and xmax,

128
00:04:41,084 --> 00:04:42,782
which might be why you've

129
00:04:42,850 --> 00:04:44,558
never heard of them.

130
00:04:45,317 --> 00:04:47,572
So with two alices on the page,

131
00:04:47,640 --> 00:04:49,824
which one does a query see?

132
00:04:49,892 --> 00:04:51,400
When a query starts,

133
00:04:51,468 --> 00:04:53,587
Postgres hands it a snapshot:

134
00:04:53,655 --> 00:04:55,760
think of it as a photo of which

135
00:04:55,828 --> 00:04:58,623
tickets had finished at that moment.

136
00:04:58,691 --> 00:05:01,282
Then one rule decides everything.

137
00:05:01,350 --> 00:05:03,523
You see a version if its creator

138
00:05:03,591 --> 00:05:05,575
had finished in your photo,

139
00:05:05,643 --> 00:05:07,555
and its deleter hadn't.

140
00:05:08,317 --> 00:05:09,957
Back to our report.

141
00:05:10,025 --> 00:05:11,655
It took its photo before

142
00:05:11,723 --> 00:05:13,363
ticket 42 finished.

143
00:05:13,431 --> 00:05:15,279
So it sees the old alice,

144
00:05:15,347 --> 00:05:17,763
100, and the old bob, 100.

145
00:05:17,831 --> 00:05:19,078
Total: 200.

146
00:05:19,146 --> 00:05:20,843
Now a second query starts

147
00:05:20,911 --> 00:05:22,823
just after 42 finishes.

148
00:05:22,891 --> 00:05:25,211
It sees alice 50 and bob 150.

149
00:05:25,278 --> 00:05:26,240
Also 200.

150
00:05:26,308 --> 00:05:28,305
Same table, same instant,

151
00:05:28,373 --> 00:05:30,017
two different answers,

152
00:05:30,085 --> 00:05:31,726
and both are right.

153
00:05:31,793 --> 00:05:33,705
That sounds like a bug.

154
00:05:33,773 --> 00:05:35,753
It's the entire feature.

155
00:05:35,821 --> 00:05:38,209
And nobody waited for anybody.

156
00:05:38,276 --> 00:05:39,649
Three out of four,

157
00:05:39,717 --> 00:05:41,561
without a single lock.

158
00:05:42,317 --> 00:05:43,607
Now say the payment

159
00:05:43,675 --> 00:05:45,723
blows up halfway through.

160
00:05:45,791 --> 00:05:47,295
You roll it back.

161
00:05:47,363 --> 00:05:50,158
And Postgres doesn't erase anything.

162
00:05:50,226 --> 00:05:51,448
It writes one line

163
00:05:51,516 --> 00:05:53,296
in a little status list:

164
00:05:53,364 --> 00:05:55,155
ticket 42, aborted.

165
00:05:55,222 --> 00:05:56,649
Everything 42 created

166
00:05:56,716 --> 00:05:58,561
now counts for nobody.

167
00:05:58,628 --> 00:06:01,491
Every deleted-by-42 stamp is ignored.

168
00:06:01,559 --> 00:06:03,679
One line, whether the payment

169
00:06:03,746 --> 00:06:06,270
touched two rows or two million.

170
00:06:06,338 --> 00:06:07,778
Honestly, I wish my

171
00:06:07,846 --> 00:06:09,961
mistakes worked like that.

172
00:06:10,029 --> 00:06:11,466
That's all four.

173
00:06:12,217 --> 00:06:15,233
All four boxes, from one hoarding habit:

174
00:06:15,301 --> 00:06:17,625
keep many versions of every row,

175
00:06:17,692 --> 00:06:19,254
and let each query pick

176
00:06:19,322 --> 00:06:21,370
the one its photo allows.

177
00:06:21,438 --> 00:06:23,747
The official name is multi-version

178
00:06:23,815 --> 00:06:25,323
concurrency control,

179
00:06:25,391 --> 00:06:28,118
a name only a committee could love.

180
00:06:28,186 --> 00:06:30,370
Everyone just says M-V-C-C.

181
00:06:30,437 --> 00:06:31,810
MySQL does it too,

182
00:06:31,878 --> 00:06:33,726
but tidier: it overwrites

183
00:06:33,793 --> 00:06:35,627
the row and stashes the old

184
00:06:35,695 --> 00:06:38,150
version in a separate undo log.

185
00:06:38,218 --> 00:06:40,945
Postgres keeps it all in the table.

186
00:06:41,013 --> 00:06:43,322
So every one of those old versions

187
00:06:43,390 --> 00:06:45,777
is still sitting on your disk.

188
00:06:46,533 --> 00:06:48,381
Once the report finishes,

189
00:06:48,449 --> 00:06:51,515
no photo anywhere can see the old alice.

190
00:06:51,583 --> 00:06:52,952
Not ever again.

191
00:06:53,020 --> 00:06:54,524
She's a dead row.

192
00:06:54,592 --> 00:06:56,086
But she's still taking

193
00:06:56,154 --> 00:06:57,930
up space on the page.

194
00:06:57,998 --> 00:07:00,589
So why not delete her right away?

195
00:07:00,657 --> 00:07:03,524
Because some photo might still need her,

196
00:07:03,591 --> 00:07:06,726
so Postgres leaves the cleanup for later.

197
00:07:06,794 --> 00:07:07,891
And delete?

198
00:07:07,959 --> 00:07:09,056
Same trick.

199
00:07:09,124 --> 00:07:11,307
It doesn't remove anything.

200
00:07:11,375 --> 00:07:14,306
It just fills in the deleted-by stamp.

201
00:07:14,374 --> 00:07:17,304
Those 10 million accounts you deleted?

202
00:07:17,372 --> 00:07:18,948
10 million dead rows,

203
00:07:19,016 --> 00:07:21,743
still sitting in your 10 gigabytes.

204
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:24,616
Which brings us to vacuum.

205
00:07:24,684 --> 00:07:26,667
If Postgres is the hoarder,

206
00:07:26,735 --> 00:07:28,987
vacuum is the cleaning crew.

207
00:07:29,055 --> 00:07:31,378
It walks the table page by page,

208
00:07:31,446 --> 00:07:34,040
finds rows no photo can see anymore,

209
00:07:34,108 --> 00:07:36,496
and marks their space as free.

210
00:07:36,564 --> 00:07:39,019
New rows move into those holes.

211
00:07:39,087 --> 00:07:40,649
And this is where Sam's

212
00:07:40,717 --> 00:07:42,832
advice disappoints people.

213
00:07:42,900 --> 00:07:44,880
The file doesn't shrink.

214
00:07:44,948 --> 00:07:47,271
You cleared out half the closet,

215
00:07:47,339 --> 00:07:50,270
and the closet is still the same size.

216
00:07:50,338 --> 00:07:51,778
Still 10 gigabytes,

217
00:07:51,846 --> 00:07:53,690
just with room inside.

218
00:07:54,450 --> 00:07:56,909
If you really want the space back,

219
00:07:56,977 --> 00:07:58,685
there's vacuum full.

220
00:07:58,753 --> 00:08:01,620
It builds a brand new copy of the table,

221
00:08:01,687 --> 00:08:03,467
with only the live rows,

222
00:08:03,535 --> 00:08:05,923
then throws the old file away.

223
00:08:05,990 --> 00:08:07,902
10 gigabytes becomes 5.

224
00:08:07,970 --> 00:08:09,329
But it's like moving

225
00:08:09,397 --> 00:08:11,308
to a smaller apartment.

226
00:08:11,376 --> 00:08:12,953
While you're packing,

227
00:08:13,020 --> 00:08:16,115
nobody gets in: no reads, no writes.

228
00:08:16,183 --> 00:08:17,895
And for a while, you're

229
00:08:17,963 --> 00:08:20,147
paying rent on both places.

230
00:08:20,215 --> 00:08:22,334
That's the bill for hoarding,

231
00:08:22,402 --> 00:08:24,857
and I won't pretend it's small.

232
00:08:25,617 --> 00:08:27,057
Luckily, you almost

233
00:08:27,125 --> 00:08:29,173
never run vacuum by hand.

234
00:08:29,241 --> 00:08:30,939
Postgres has a background

235
00:08:31,007 --> 00:08:33,054
helper called autovacuum.

236
00:08:33,122 --> 00:08:34,956
It wakes up whenever enough

237
00:08:35,024 --> 00:08:37,140
rows in a table have died.

238
00:08:37,207 --> 00:08:38,987
But it has one weakness,

239
00:08:39,055 --> 00:08:40,628
and it's a person.

240
00:08:40,696 --> 00:08:42,475
Somewhere, someone typed

241
00:08:42,543 --> 00:08:44,052
BEGIN in a terminal,

242
00:08:44,120 --> 00:08:46,996
went to lunch, and never came back.

243
00:08:47,064 --> 00:08:48,626
That forgotten BEGIN is

244
00:08:48,694 --> 00:08:50,946
still holding its old photo.

245
00:08:51,014 --> 00:08:53,119
And vacuum won't clean anything

246
00:08:53,187 --> 00:08:55,370
that photo might still see.

247
00:08:55,438 --> 00:08:57,079
So the table grows.

248
00:08:57,147 --> 00:08:58,176
And grows.

249
00:08:58,244 --> 00:08:59,273
And grows.

250
00:08:59,341 --> 00:09:01,728
Disk filling up for no reason?

251
00:09:01,796 --> 00:09:04,523
Look for the forgotten BEGIN first.

252
00:09:05,283 --> 00:09:07,063
Vacuum has one more job,

253
00:09:07,131 --> 00:09:09,043
and it's the scary one.

254
00:09:09,111 --> 00:09:12,109
Those deli tickets are a 32-bit number.

255
00:09:12,177 --> 00:09:13,821
After about 4 billion,

256
00:09:13,889 --> 00:09:16,616
they wrap back around to the start.

257
00:09:16,684 --> 00:09:19,479
So Postgres reads them like a clock.

258
00:09:19,547 --> 00:09:22,138
2 billion behind now is the past.

259
00:09:22,206 --> 00:09:24,593
2 billion ahead is the future.

260
00:09:24,661 --> 00:09:26,848
Leave a row alone long enough,

261
00:09:26,916 --> 00:09:28,614
and its created-by ticket

262
00:09:28,682 --> 00:09:30,394
drifts into the future,

263
00:09:30,462 --> 00:09:33,460
and a row from the future is invisible.

264
00:09:33,528 --> 00:09:35,780
Your data would just vanish.

265
00:09:35,848 --> 00:09:37,831
So vacuum freezes old rows:

266
00:09:37,899 --> 00:09:40,630
it stamps them as visible to everyone,

267
00:09:40,698 --> 00:09:41,591
forever.

268
00:09:41,659 --> 00:09:44,186
And if vacuum falls too far behind,

269
00:09:44,254 --> 00:09:46,577
Postgres stops accepting writes,

270
00:09:46,645 --> 00:09:49,440
rather than let your data disappear.

271
00:09:49,508 --> 00:09:51,487
This isn't hypothetical.

272
00:09:51,555 --> 00:09:54,150
In 2015, Sentry was down for most of

273
00:09:54,218 --> 00:09:57,149
a working day because of exactly this.

274
00:09:57,217 --> 00:09:58,110
Painful.

275
00:09:58,178 --> 00:09:59,740
But I'd still rather my

276
00:09:59,808 --> 00:10:02,127
database stop than lie to me.

277
00:10:02,883 --> 00:10:05,285
So, putting it all together.

278
00:10:05,352 --> 00:10:07,808
An update writes a new version.

279
00:10:07,876 --> 00:10:10,059
A delete stamps an old one.

280
00:10:10,127 --> 00:10:13,194
Every query reads through its own photo.

281
00:10:13,262 --> 00:10:14,620
And vacuum sweeps up

282
00:10:14,688 --> 00:10:16,803
whatever no photo can see.

283
00:10:16,871 --> 00:10:19,191
Postgres never changes a row.

284
00:10:19,259 --> 00:10:20,767
It writes a new one,

285
00:10:20,835 --> 00:10:23,769
which is why readers never block writers,

286
00:10:23,837 --> 00:10:25,467
and why your table grows

287
00:10:25,535 --> 00:10:27,447
until vacuum cleans up.

288
00:10:28,200 --> 00:10:30,737
So, back to your 10 gigabytes.

289
00:10:30,805 --> 00:10:32,096
Your delete stamped

290
00:10:32,163 --> 00:10:34,143
10 million rows as dead.

291
00:10:34,211 --> 00:10:37,074
Autovacuum marks their space as free.

292
00:10:37,142 --> 00:10:39,043
Your next 10 million inserts

293
00:10:39,111 --> 00:10:40,755
move into those holes,

294
00:10:40,823 --> 00:10:43,414
and the file doesn't grow at all.

295
00:10:43,482 --> 00:10:46,077
And if you truly need the disk back,

296
00:10:46,145 --> 00:10:48,761
run vacuum full at 3 a.m.,

297
00:10:48,829 --> 00:10:50,673
when nobody's looking.

298
00:10:50,741 --> 00:10:53,006
Sam was right, by the way.

299
00:10:53,074 --> 00:10:55,462
Sam is annoyingly often right.

300
00:10:56,217 --> 00:10:58,876
Want to catch Postgres in the act?

301
00:10:58,944 --> 00:11:00,738
Run select xmin, xmax,

302
00:11:00,806 --> 00:11:02,446
star from accounts.

303
00:11:02,514 --> 00:11:04,348
Those are hidden columns on

304
00:11:04,416 --> 00:11:06,735
every table you've ever made.

305
00:11:06,803 --> 00:11:08,937
Update a row, run it again,

306
00:11:09,005 --> 00:11:11,935
and the created-by number has changed.

307
00:11:12,003 --> 00:11:14,390
It's not the same row anymore.

308
00:11:14,458 --> 00:11:16,302
It's a brand new copy.

309
00:11:16,370 --> 00:11:18,282
Postgres never forgets.

310
00:11:18,350 --> 00:11:20,534
It just eventually lets go.

311
00:11:20,602 --> 00:11:22,300
Have you ever been bitten

312
00:11:22,367 --> 00:11:24,144
by a forgotten BEGIN?

313
00:11:24,211 --> 00:11:27,006
How long did it take you to find it?

314
00:11:27,767 --> 00:11:30,169
So, to summarise everything.

315
00:11:30,236 --> 00:11:32,624
An update never changes a row.

316
00:11:32,692 --> 00:11:34,472
It writes a new version,

317
00:11:34,539 --> 00:11:36,451
and stamps the old one.

318
00:11:36,519 --> 00:11:38,217
Every query reads through

319
00:11:38,285 --> 00:11:39,590
its own snapshot,

320
00:11:39,657 --> 00:11:41,152
so readers and writers

321
00:11:41,219 --> 00:11:43,335
never wait for each other.

322
00:11:43,403 --> 00:11:45,251
A delete is just a stamp,

323
00:11:45,319 --> 00:11:46,949
and vacuum is what turns

324
00:11:47,017 --> 00:11:49,472
dead rows back into free space.

325
00:11:49,540 --> 00:11:51,388
Leave a transaction open,

326
00:11:51,455 --> 00:11:53,371
or let vacuum fall behind,

327
00:11:53,439 --> 00:11:55,147
and the table grows.

328
00:11:55,215 --> 00:11:57,059
Postgres is a hoarder.

329
00:11:57,127 --> 00:11:58,224
On purpose.

330
00:11:58,983 --> 00:12:00,695
If you liked the video,

331
00:12:00,763 --> 00:12:02,679
drop a like and subscribe,

332
00:12:02,746 --> 00:12:04,308
and tell me what topics

333
00:12:04,376 --> 00:12:06,153
you want to see next.
